{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-80e061e9b54bc91e", "type": "tool_result", "content": "(4)\n\nBy Theorem 3.1, the scalar constraint in Equation (3) is realized\nby row-wise scalar Möbius multiplication, allowing the Euclidean\nscalar 𝑠 (ℓ ) to be optimized via standard AdamW while strictly realizing the intended radial displacement. However, a single scalar\n𝑠 (ℓ ) applies uniform radial scaling to all rows and all feature dimensions, which is too rigid for adaptation across heterogeneous\nencoder channels. Different layers of a frozen audio encoder capture emotion cues at different hierarchical levels: shallow layers\nencode acoustic templates such as pitch contour and energy envelope, while deep layers distill emotion-discriminative semantics.\nForcing a uniform adaptation across this hierarchy is therefore suboptimal. Our solution is to generalize the scalar rule to a diagonal\n𝑎\nmodulation matrix W𝑠(ℓ ) = diag(s (ℓ ) ) with s (ℓ ) ∈ R𝑑>0\n, which acts\nas a layer-adaptive granularity controller: by learning per-layer,\nper-dimension scaling factors, HGA adjusts the radial position\nof representations layer by layer, explicitly aligning each layer’s\nadaptation with the encoder’s intrinsic hierarchy. The resulting\n\n3.3.2 HGA Injection into the Audio Encoder. HyPASE injects HGA\nonly into the query and value projections of each self-attention\n𝑄/𝑉 ,(ℓ )\nlayer, replacing the frozen weights W0\nwith hyperbolic-modulated\n𝑄/𝑉 ,(ℓ )\n)\nWHGA (Equation (5)) while leaving the key projection W𝐾,(ℓ\nun0\nchanged. The asymmetric design is deliberate: K determines where\nthe model attends (the pre-trained attention anchor), while Q and\nV determine at what granularity the model queries and extracts\n4\n\n\fHyPASE: Hyperbolic Geometry for Parameter-Efficient Speech Emotion Fine-Tuning Framework for Large Audio-Language Models\n\nthe task-conditioned audio summary ã:\n\ninformation. Freezing K preserves the encoder’s original attention\npatterns; modulating Q/V shifts the representational granularity of\nwhat is queried and extracted, aligning with the goal of granularityaware adaptation without altering the encoder’s attention structure.\nEach encoder layer additionally carries a lightweight bottleneck\nresidual adapter as a Euclidean residual branch, providing local nonlinear adaptation that complements HGA’s hyperbolic weight-space\nmodulation.\n\n𝑇\n\nā =\n\nh (𝑖 ) = 𝜙𝑖 ( ã), 𝑖 ∈ {1, 2, 3},\n\n𝜶 = softmax(Wroute ã),\n\n\u0001\n𝑑ℎ\n𝐷,𝑐\n𝐷,𝑐\n(1)\n(2)\n(3)\npfuse = EinMid exp𝐷,𝑐\n0 (h ), exp0 (h ), exp0 (h ); 𝜶 , 𝑐 ∈ D𝑐 ,\nu = log𝐷,𝑐\n0 (pfuse ).\n\n(9)\n\nEinMid is computed via the standard Klein-to-Poincaré back-projection [14].\nBranches capturing more discriminative emotion features lie\ncloser to the Poincaré ball boundary; the Einstein midpoint assigns\nhigher implicit weight to these boundary-proximal points via the\nLorentz factor 𝛾𝑖 = (1 − 𝑐 ∥k𝑖 ∥ 2 ) −1/2 (where k𝑖 is the Klein image of\np𝑖 ): as a point approaches the boundary, its Lorentz factor grows,\nincreasing its contribution to the midpoint. This geometry-native\nweighting mechanism automatically prioritizes fine-grained emotional semantics during fusion, a property absent in flat Euclidean\naveraging, which weights branches purely by the learned scalar\n𝜶 without geometric amplification. Ablation rows G1 and G3 in\nTable 2 confirm that replacing hyperbolic fusion with Euclidean\naveraging degrades performance by up to 4.08 pp WA.\n\n3.4.1 Overall EMCA Pipeline. Given audio hidden states A ∈ R𝑇 ×𝑑𝑎\nand the instruction prompt embeddings T ∈ R𝑁 ×𝑑𝑡 (token embeddings of the fixed emotion classification prompt), EMCA produces\naudio prefixes via:\n.\n\n(8)\n\n3.4.3 Multi-Capacity Hyperbolic Integration. Three MLP branches\nwith bottleneck ratios 1/8, 1/16, and 1/32 extract features at decreasing capacity levels from ã in parallel, then map them to the Poincaré\nball and fuse them via the Einstein midpoint operator, which better preserves radial distinctions among branches than Euclidean\nweighted averaging. The branch weights 𝜶 = softmax(Wroute ã) ∈\nΔ2 are input-adaptively computed from the task-conditioned summary ã via a learnable routing matrix Wroute ∈ R3×𝑑𝑎 , endowing the\nfusion with dynamic branch selection rather than fixed importance\nweights:\n\nEMCA is a task-level lightweight aggregator that compresses the\nutterance-level hidden states produced by HGA into audio prefixes\nfor the frozen LLM. Unlike HGA, which adapts the encoder weights\nin hyperbolic space, EMCA operates as an utterance-level semantic\nreadout module. Its role is to summarize frame representations, apply task-conditioned gating, and fuse multiple bottleneck branches\nbefore prefix construction.\n\n\u0001\u0001\n\n1 ∑︁\nT𝑛 ,\n𝑁 𝑛=1\n\nTemporal structure is modeled primarily within the audio encoder, where HGA modulates weights across layers and self-attention\ncaptures frame-level dependencies throughout the 𝐿 layers; EMCA\ncomplements this by aggregating the resulting frame representations into a compact utterance-level summary, with semantic\nabstraction diversity provided by its three bottleneck branches\n(Section 3.4.3).\n\nEmotion-aware Multi-capacity Cross-modal\nAggregator (EMCA)\n\nZaudio = Π HypFuse 𝜙 1 ( ã), 𝜙 2 ( ã), 𝜙 3 ( ã)\n\nt̄ =\n\nã = ā ⊙ (Wtext t̄ + btext ).\n\n3.3.3 Parameter Efficiency Analysis. Under the diagonal parameterization, the Q/V projections of HGA at layer ℓ introduce 2𝑑𝑎\ndiagonal scaling parameters. Combined with a residual adapter of\nbottleneck dimension 𝑟 (up/down projections each of size 𝑑𝑎 × 𝑟 ),\nthe parameter count per layer is 2𝑑𝑎 +2𝑑𝑎 𝑟 = 2𝑑𝑎 (1+𝑟 ). For Qwen2Audio with 32 layers, hidden dimension 1280, and 𝑟 = 12, HGA\nacross all layers requires approximately 1.06M trainable parameters,\nonly 0.013% of the full Qwen2-Audio-7B parameter count. Adding\nEMCA (approximately 5.35M) and the base model’s native multimodal projector, the total trainable parameters of HyPASE amount\nto approximately 0.12% of Qwen2-Audio-7B. All other parameter\nefficiency figures reported in this paper refer to the full framework,\nnot HGA alone.\n\n3.4\n\n𝑁\n\n1 ∑︁\nA𝑡 ,\n𝑇 𝑡 =1\n\n(7)\n\nwhere HypFuse(·) denotes the three-branch hyperbolic fusion (Section 3.4.3). The three branches share a common summary vector\nand extract complementary emotion representations at different\nbottleneck capacities.\n\n3.4.4 Audio Prefix Construction and Parameter Efficiency. The fusion vector u is back-projected and expanded by prefix generator\nΠ into 𝐾 audio prefix tokens, then projected by the multimodal\nprojector MMP to the LLM input dimension:\n\n3.4.2 Task-Conditioned Gating. Since HyPASE operates on pure\naudio without ASR transcription, the instruction prompt serves not\nas text semantics but as a task-conditioned gate. Speech signals contain substantial emotion-irrelevant acoustic detail (channel noise,\nspeaker identity artifacts, and recording conditions); element-wise\ngating between the pooled prompt embedding t̄ and the audio summary activates channels relevant to the queried emotion categories\nwhile suppressing task-irrelevant noise. We implement this via\nHadamard multiplication, introducing only one 𝑑𝑎 × 𝑑𝑡 projection\nmatrix and one 𝑑𝑎 bias vector-about one-third the parameter cost of\ncross-attention. EMCA first applies global average pooling to audio\nand prompt sequences to obtain summary vectors, then produces\n\nuaudio = Wproj u + bproj,\nZaudio = Π(uaudio ),\nHaudio = MMP(Zaudio ; 𝜃 proj ).\n\n(10)\n\nThe prefix generator is defined as Π(uaudio ) = Qpfx + 1𝐾 u⊤\naudio ∈\nR𝐾 ×𝑑𝑎 , where Qpfx ∈ R𝐾 ×𝑑𝑎 is a learnable prefix query matrix, 1𝐾 ∈\nR𝐾 is the all-ones vector, and uaudio ∈ R𝑑𝑎 is the back-projected\nfusion vector.\n5\n\n\fTian et al.\n\n3.5\n\nTraining Objective\n\nfrom EMO-RL [21] is listed in Table 1 for scale only, given its different training regime.\n\nHyPASE jointly optimizes 𝜃 HGA , 𝜃 EMCA , and 𝜃 proj via a composite\nloss:\nL = LCE + 𝜆hyp Lhyp + 𝜆radius Lradius,\n(11)\nwhere LCE is the standard label cross-entropy loss. Lhyp applies\ncross-entropy on geodesic distances from the EMCA fusion point\n𝑑\npfuse ∈ D𝑐 ℎ (Section 3.4.3) to learnable class prototypes. Each\nprototype v𝑘 ∈ R𝑑ℎ is parameterized in the tangent space at the\norigin and projected onto the Poincaré ball during the forward pass,\nensuring numerical stability under standard AdamW optimization:\n\u0010n\n\u0010\n\u0011o\n\u0011\nLhyp = CE − 𝜏1 𝑑𝑐 pfuse, exp𝐷,𝑐\n,𝑦 .\n(12)\n0 (v𝑘 )\n\nImplementation Details. The backbone is Qwen2-Audio-7B-Instruct.\nAll modules are trained in BFloat16 precision on 4 NVIDIA RTX\n4090 24G GPUs. The optimizer is AdamW with learning rate 1.125×\n10−3 and batch size 32 (via gradient accumulation). All experiments\nare trained for 30 epochs with an early stopping strategy of patience\n= 5, using validation WA to select the best checkpoint. Default hyperparameters: curvature 𝑐 = 0.01, HGA bottleneck dimension\n𝑟 = 12, EMCA hidden dimension 𝑑ℎ = 256, number of audio prefix\ntokens 𝐾 = 4, auxiliary loss weights 𝜆hyp = 0.1 and 𝜆radius = 0.01,\nradius ranking margin 𝑚 = 0.5, hyperbolic prototype loss temperature 𝜏 = 0.07. The emotion classification instruction prompt x𝑡\nfollows a fixed template listing the target emotion categories for\neach dataset (e.g., “Identify the emotion from: neutral, surprise, fear,\nsadness, joy, disgust, anger” for MELD); this prompt is shared across\nall training samples of a given dataset.\n\n𝑘\n\nSince exp𝐷,𝑐\n0 maps any finite v𝑘 strictly inside the ball, and pfuse\n(as an Einstein midpoint of exp-mapped branch outputs) also lies\nstrictly inside, pairwise geodesic distances remain bounded and\ntemperature-scaled logits (𝜏 = 0.07) stay numerically stable.\nLradius enforces a monotonic radius ordering across the three\nEMCA branches (high → low capacity) via a hyperbolic margin\nranking formulation:\n2\n∑︁\n\nEvaluation Metrics. Following domain conventions [21], three\nmetrics are reported: Weighted Accuracy (WA), which measures\noverall classification accuracy; Unweighted Accuracy (UA), which\ncomputes the equal-weight average over all classes and better\nreflects minority class performance on imbalanced datasets; and\nmacro-averaged F1 (F1), which provides a comprehensive evaluation insensitive to class distribution.\n\n𝐵\n1 ∑︁\n(𝑖 ) \u0001\nRad exp𝐷,𝑐\n0 (h𝑏 ) ,\n𝐵\n𝑖=1\n𝑏=1\n(13)\nwhere h𝑏(𝑖 ) = 𝜙𝑖 ( ã𝑏 ) ∈ R𝑑ℎ is the tangent-space MLP output of\nbranch 𝑖 for sample 𝑏.\nLradius enforces structural ordering among the three EMCA\nbranches, ensuring that higher-capacity branches occupy larger\nradii than lower-capacity ones. Lhyp anchors this ordering to emotion semantics by pulling pfuse toward the correct class prototype.\nCombining both losses ensures that radial structure is aligned with\nemotion discriminability; either loss alone is insufficient, as confirmed by ablation rows L1 and L2 in Table 2.\n\nLradius =\n\n\u0001\nmax 0, 𝑚−(¯𝑟𝑖 −¯𝑟𝑖+1 ) ,\n\n𝑟¯𝑖 =\n\n4.2\n\nMain Results\n\nUnder a 0.12% parameter budget, HyPASE outperforms both Euclidean PEFT baselines on MELD across all three metrics, with the\nlargest gain on F1 (+5.48 pp over LoRA). On IEMOCAP, an instructive trade-off emerges: WA is marginally below LoRA (−1.52 pp)\nwhile UA rises by +3.23 pp, indicating that hyperbolic geometry redistributes representational capacity away from majority classes toward minority ones. This is consistent with the geometric prior: hyperbolic space allocates exponentially more volume at larger radii,\nnaturally counteracting the feature compression that Euclidean\nPEFT imposes on underrepresented classes.\n\n4 Experiments\n4.1 Experimental Setup\nDatasets. Main experiments are conducted on two standard SER\nbenchmarks: IEMOCAP [3] (4-class, LOSO cross-validation) and\nMELD [31] (7-class, standard train/dev/test split). The cross-dataset\ngeneralization experiment (Section 4.4) transfers MELD-trained\nmodels to IEMOCAP, RAVDESS [24], and SAVEE [16] in a zeroshot manner, and compares HyPASE against the Qwen2-Audio\nzero-shot baseline under the same WA metric.\n\n4.3\n\nAblation Study\n\nAll ablation experiments report WA/UA/F1 on the MELD test set.\nTable 2 decomposes component contributions along the module,\nloss, and geometry dimensions.\nGeometry effect. Comparing M1 (Hyperbolic HGA only) with G2\n(Euclidean HGA only), both using LCE and no EMCA, isolates the\neffect of the parameter space. Hyperbolic weight-space modulation\nyields +2.09 pp WA (66.16 vs. 64.07), indicating a stronger inductive\nbias for audio encoder adaptation than its Euclidean counterpart.\n\nBaselines. We compare against baselines at three levels, following [21]. This single-backbone, MELD+IEMOCAP evaluation protocol is the prevailing paradigm in LALM-based SER, adopted by\nrecent works including EMO-RL [21] and EmoAgent-R1 [12], ensuring cross-study comparability. Non-LALM methods include HuBERT large [18], WavLM large [6], data2vec 2.0 large [1], Whisper\nlarge V3 [32], and Emotion2vec+ large [26], all fine-tuned with classification heads; results are taken from EmoBox [25]. LALM zeroshot baselines use Qwen2-Audio with direct inference and chainof-thought prompting. Euclidean PEFT baselines use LoRA [19]\nand Adapter [17] on the same Qwen2-Audio backbone with identical training configurations as HyPASE, each with approximately\n10.49M trainable parameters. The full-parameter SFT+IR reference\n\nGeometric supervision effect. Adding EMCA to HGA without\ngeometric losses (L0, 63.90) drops below HGA-only performance\n(M1, 66.16). Without radius ordering supervision, the three parallel\nbranches produce redundant representations that interfere with\nHGA’s outputs. Among individual loss terms, Lradius (L2, 65.61)\ncontributes the most by establishing the branch-separation prior;\nLhyp (L1, 64.17) alone provides marginal gain but combines with\nLradius to anchor the ordered structure to emotion semantics. The\n6\n\n\fHyPASE: Hyperbolic Geometry for Parameter-Efficient Speech Emotion Fine-Tuning Framework for Large Audio-Language Models\n\nTable 1: Comparison on MELD (7-class) and IEMOCAP (4-class) (%). Non-LALM results from EmoBox [25]. LALM+PEFT methods\nshare identical training configurations. †: full-parameter reference from [21]. Bold: best PEFT.\nModel Type\n\nMELD\n\nMethod\nUA\n\nWA\n\nF1\n\nUA\n\nWA\n\nF1\n\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\n\nHuBERT large\nWavLM large\ndata2vec 2.0 large\nWhisper large V3\nEmotion2vec+ large\n\nNon-LALM Methods\n24.13\n46.37\n28.18\n49.31\n26.33\n47.72\n31.54\n51.89\n28.03\n51.88\n\n24.99\n29.11\n27.35\n32.95\n–\n\n67.42\n69.47\n57.30\n73.54\n70.70\n\n66.69\n69.07\n56.23\n72.86\n67.30\n\n67.24\n69.29\n56.70\n73.11\n–\n\nLALM\nLALM\nLALM-FT†\n\nLALM Methods\nQwen2-Audio (Direct Inference)\n18.96\n39.83\nQwen2-Audio (CoT Inference)\n26.89\n50.57\nSFT + IR [21]\n33.26\n57.39\n\n19.84\n28.05\n35.77\n\n53.76\n64.33\n85.70\n\n51.52\n60.37\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "41299264-c8c0-4524-982f-0fa7c1d80884", "uuid": "001f0586-057e-492e-b96e-c724e212cb9c", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T06:27:50.488Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "(4)\n\nBy Theorem 3.1, the scalar constraint in Equation (3) is realized\nby row-wise scalar Möbius multiplication, allowing the Euclidean\nscalar 𝑠 (ℓ ) to be optimized via standard AdamW while strictly realizing the intended radial displacement. However, a single scalar\n𝑠 (ℓ ) applies uniform radial scaling to all rows and all feature dimensions, which is too rigid for adaptation across heterogeneous\nencoder channels. Different layers of a frozen audio encoder capture emotion cues at different hierarchical levels: shallow layers\nencode acoustic templates such as pitch contour and energy envelope, while deep layers distill emotion-discriminative semantics.\nForcing a uniform adaptation across this hierarchy is therefore suboptimal. Our solution is to generalize the scalar rule to a diagonal\n𝑎\nmodulation matrix W𝑠(ℓ ) = diag(s (ℓ ) ) with s (ℓ ) ∈ R𝑑>0\n, which acts\nas a layer-adaptive granularity controller: by learning per-layer,\nper-dimension scaling factors, HGA adjusts the radial position\nof representations layer by layer, explicitly aligning each layer’s\nadaptation with the encoder’s intrinsic hierarchy. The resulting\n\n3.3.2 HGA Injection into the Audio Encoder. HyPASE injects HGA\nonly into the query and value projections of each self-attention\n𝑄/𝑉 ,(ℓ )\nlayer, replacing the frozen weights W0\nwith hyperbolic-modulated\n𝑄/𝑉 ,(ℓ )\n)\nWHGA (Equation (5)) while leaving the key projection W𝐾,(ℓ\nun0\nchanged. The asymmetric design is deliberate: K determines where\nthe model attends (the pre-trained attention anchor), while Q and\nV determine at what granularity the model queries and extracts\n4\n\n\fHyPASE: Hyperbolic Geometry for Parameter-Efficient Speech Emotion Fine-Tuning Framework for Large Audio-Language Models\n\nthe task-conditioned audio summary ã:\n\ninformation. Freezing K preserves the encoder’s original attention\npatterns; modulating Q/V shifts the representational granularity of\nwhat is queried and extracted, aligning with the goal of granularityaware adaptation without altering the encoder’s attention structure.\nEach encoder layer additionally carries a lightweight bottleneck\nresidual adapter as a Euclidean residual branch, providing local nonlinear adaptation that complements HGA’s hyperbolic weight-space\nmodulation.\n\n𝑇\n\nā =\n\nh (𝑖 ) = 𝜙𝑖 ( ã), 𝑖 ∈ {1, 2, 3},\n\n𝜶 = softmax(Wroute ã),\n\n\u0001\n𝑑ℎ\n𝐷,𝑐\n𝐷,𝑐\n(1)\n(2)\n(3)\npfuse = EinMid exp𝐷,𝑐\n0 (h ), exp0 (h ), exp0 (h ); 𝜶 , 𝑐 ∈ D𝑐 ,\nu = log𝐷,𝑐\n0 (pfuse ).\n\n(9)\n\nEinMid is computed via the standard Klein-to-Poincaré back-projection [14].\nBranches capturing more discriminative emotion features lie\ncloser to the Poincaré ball boundary; the Einstein midpoint assigns\nhigher implicit weight to these boundary-proximal points via the\nLorentz factor 𝛾𝑖 = (1 − 𝑐 ∥k𝑖 ∥ 2 ) −1/2 (where k𝑖 is the Klein image of\np𝑖 ): as a point approaches the boundary, its Lorentz factor grows,\nincreasing its contribution to the midpoint. This geometry-native\nweighting mechanism automatically prioritizes fine-grained emotional semantics during fusion, a property absent in flat Euclidean\naveraging, which weights branches purely by the learned scalar\n𝜶 without geometric amplification. Ablation rows G1 and G3 in\nTable 2 confirm that replacing hyperbolic fusion with Euclidean\naveraging degrades performance by up to 4.08 pp WA.\n\n3.4.1 Overall EMCA Pipeline. Given audio hidden states A ∈ R𝑇 ×𝑑𝑎\nand the instruction prompt embeddings T ∈ R𝑁 ×𝑑𝑡 (token embeddings of the fixed emotion classification prompt), EMCA produces\naudio prefixes via:\n.\n\n(8)\n\n3.4.3 Multi-Capacity Hyperbolic Integration. Three MLP branches\nwith bottleneck ratios 1/8, 1/16, and 1/32 extract features at decreasing capacity levels from ã in parallel, then map them to the Poincaré\nball and fuse them via the Einstein midpoint operator, which better preserves radial distinctions among branches than Euclidean\nweighted averaging. The branch weights 𝜶 = softmax(Wroute ã) ∈\nΔ2 are input-adaptively computed from the task-conditioned summary ã via a learnable routing matrix Wroute ∈ R3×𝑑𝑎 , endowing the\nfusion with dynamic branch selection rather than fixed importance\nweights:\n\nEMCA is a task-level lightweight aggregator that compresses the\nutterance-level hidden states produced by HGA into audio prefixes\nfor the frozen LLM. Unlike HGA, which adapts the encoder weights\nin hyperbolic space, EMCA operates as an utterance-level semantic\nreadout module. Its role is to summarize frame representations, apply task-conditioned gating, and fuse multiple bottleneck branches\nbefore prefix construction.\n\n\u0001\u0001\n\n1 ∑︁\nT𝑛 ,\n𝑁 𝑛=1\n\nTemporal structure is modeled primarily within the audio encoder, where HGA modulates weights across layers and self-attention\ncaptures frame-level dependencies throughout the 𝐿 layers; EMCA\ncomplements this by aggregating the resulting frame representations into a compact utterance-level summary, with semantic\nabstraction diversity provided by its three bottleneck branches\n(Section 3.4.3).\n\nEmotion-aware Multi-capacity Cross-modal\nAggregator (EMCA)\n\nZaudio = Π HypFuse 𝜙 1 ( ã), 𝜙 2 ( ã), 𝜙 3 ( ã)\n\nt̄ =\n\nã = ā ⊙ (Wtext t̄ + btext ).\n\n3.3.3 Parameter Efficiency Analysis. Under the diagonal parameterization, the Q/V projections of HGA at layer ℓ introduce 2𝑑𝑎\ndiagonal scaling parameters. Combined with a residual adapter of\nbottleneck dimension 𝑟 (up/down projections each of size 𝑑𝑎 × 𝑟 ),\nthe parameter count per layer is 2𝑑𝑎 +2𝑑𝑎 𝑟 = 2𝑑𝑎 (1+𝑟 ). For Qwen2Audio with 32 layers, hidden dimension 1280, and 𝑟 = 12, HGA\nacross all layers requires approximately 1.06M trainable parameters,\nonly 0.013% of the full Qwen2-Audio-7B parameter count. Adding\nEMCA (approximately 5.35M) and the base model’s native multimodal projector, the total trainable parameters of HyPASE amount\nto approximately 0.12% of Qwen2-Audio-7B. All other parameter\nefficiency figures reported in this paper refer to the full framework,\nnot HGA alone.\n\n3.4\n\n𝑁\n\n1 ∑︁\nA𝑡 ,\n𝑇 𝑡 =1\n\n(7)\n\nwhere HypFuse(·) denotes the three-branch hyperbolic fusion (Section 3.4.3). The three branches share a common summary vector\nand extract complementary emotion representations at different\nbottleneck capacities.\n\n3.4.4 Audio Prefix Construction and Parameter Efficiency. The fusion vector u is back-projected and expanded by prefix generator\nΠ into 𝐾 audio prefix tokens, then projected by the multimodal\nprojector MMP to the LLM input dimension:\n\n3.4.2 Task-Conditioned Gating. Since HyPASE operates on pure\naudio without ASR transcription, the instruction prompt serves not\nas text semantics but as a task-conditioned gate. Speech signals contain substantial emotion-irrelevant acoustic detail (channel noise,\nspeaker identity artifacts, and recording conditions); element-wise\ngating between the pooled prompt embedding t̄ and the audio summary activates channels relevant to the queried emotion categories\nwhile suppressing task-irrelevant noise. We implement this via\nHadamard multiplication, introducing only one 𝑑𝑎 × 𝑑𝑡 projection\nmatrix and one 𝑑𝑎 bias vector-about one-third the parameter cost of\ncross-attention. EMCA first applies global average pooling to audio\nand prompt sequences to obtain summary vectors, then produces\n\nuaudio = Wproj u + bproj,\nZaudio = Π(uaudio ),\nHaudio = MMP(Zaudio ; 𝜃 proj ).\n\n(10)\n\nThe prefix generator is defined as Π(uaudio ) = Qpfx + 1𝐾 u⊤\naudio ∈\nR𝐾 ×𝑑𝑎 , where Qpfx ∈ R𝐾 ×𝑑𝑎 is a learnable prefix query matrix, 1𝐾 ∈\nR𝐾 is the all-ones vector, and uaudio ∈ R𝑑𝑎 is the back-projected\nfusion vector.\n5\n\n\fTian et al.\n\n3.5\n\nTraining Objective\n\nfrom EMO-RL [21] is listed in Table 1 for scale only, given its different training regime.\n\nHyPASE jointly optimizes 𝜃 HGA , 𝜃 EMCA , and 𝜃 proj via a composite\nloss:\nL = LCE + 𝜆hyp Lhyp + 𝜆radius Lradius,\n(11)\nwhere LCE is the standard label cross-entropy loss. Lhyp applies\ncross-entropy on geodesic distances from the EMCA fusion point\n𝑑\npfuse ∈ D𝑐 ℎ (Section 3.4.3) to learnable class prototypes. Each\nprototype v𝑘 ∈ R𝑑ℎ is parameterized in the tangent space at the\norigin and projected onto the Poincaré ball during the forward pass,\nensuring numerical stability under standard AdamW optimization:\n\u0010n\n\u0010\n\u0011o\n\u0011\nLhyp = CE − 𝜏1 𝑑𝑐 pfuse, exp𝐷,𝑐\n,𝑦 .\n(12)\n0 (v𝑘 )\n\nImplementation Details. The backbone is Qwen2-Audio-7B-Instruct.\nAll modules are trained in BFloat16 precision on 4 NVIDIA RTX\n4090 24G GPUs. The optimizer is AdamW with learning rate 1.125×\n10−3 and batch size 32 (via gradient accumulation). All experiments\nare trained for 30 epochs with an early stopping strategy of patience\n= 5, using validation WA to select the best checkpoint. Default hyperparameters: curvature 𝑐 = 0.01, HGA bottleneck dimension\n𝑟 = 12, EMCA hidden dimension 𝑑ℎ = 256, number of audio prefix\ntokens 𝐾 = 4, auxiliary loss weights 𝜆hyp = 0.1 and 𝜆radius = 0.01,\nradius ranking margin 𝑚 = 0.5, hyperbolic prototype loss temperature 𝜏 = 0.07. The emotion classification instruction prompt x𝑡\nfollows a fixed template listing the target emotion categories for\neach dataset (e.g., “Identify the emotion from: neutral, surprise, fear,\nsadness, joy, disgust, anger” for MELD); this prompt is shared across\nall training samples of a given dataset.\n\n𝑘\n\nSince exp𝐷,𝑐\n0 maps any finite v𝑘 strictly inside the ball, and pfuse\n(as an Einstein midpoint of exp-mapped branch outputs) also lies\nstrictly inside, pairwise geodesic distances remain bounded and\ntemperature-scaled logits (𝜏 = 0.07) stay numerically stable.\nLradius enforces a monotonic radius ordering across the three\nEMCA branches (high → low capacity) via a hyperbolic margin\nranking formulation:\n2\n∑︁\n\nEvaluation Metrics. Following domain conventions [21], three\nmetrics are reported: Weighted Accuracy (WA), which measures\noverall classification accuracy; Unweighted Accuracy (UA), which\ncomputes the equal-weight average over all classes and better\nreflects minority class performance on imbalanced datasets; and\nmacro-averaged F1 (F1), which provides a comprehensive evaluation insensitive to class distribution.\n\n𝐵\n1 ∑︁\n(𝑖 ) \u0001\nRad exp𝐷,𝑐\n0 (h𝑏 ) ,\n𝐵\n𝑖=1\n𝑏=1\n(13)\nwhere h𝑏(𝑖 ) = 𝜙𝑖 ( ã𝑏 ) ∈ R𝑑ℎ is the tangent-space MLP output of\nbranch 𝑖 for sample 𝑏.\nLradius enforces structural ordering among the three EMCA\nbranches, ensuring that higher-capacity branches occupy larger\nradii than lower-capacity ones. Lhyp anchors this ordering to emotion semantics by pulling pfuse toward the correct class prototype.\nCombining both losses ensures that radial structure is aligned with\nemotion discriminability; either loss alone is insufficient, as confirmed by ablation rows L1 and L2 in Table 2.\n\nLradius =\n\n\u0001\nmax 0, 𝑚−(¯𝑟𝑖 −¯𝑟𝑖+1 ) ,\n\n𝑟¯𝑖 =\n\n4.2\n\nMain Results\n\nUnder a 0.12% parameter budget, HyPASE outperforms both Euclidean PEFT baselines on MELD across all three metrics, with the\nlargest gain on F1 (+5.48 pp over LoRA). On IEMOCAP, an instructive trade-off emerges: WA is marginally below LoRA (−1.52 pp)\nwhile UA rises by +3.23 pp, indicating that hyperbolic geometry redistributes representational capacity away from majority classes toward minority ones. This is consistent with the geometric prior: hyperbolic space allocates exponentially more volume at larger radii,\nnaturally counteracting the feature compression that Euclidean\nPEFT imposes on underrepresented classes.\n\n4 Experiments\n4.1 Experimental Setup\nDatasets. Main experiments are conducted on two standard SER\nbenchmarks: IEMOCAP [3] (4-class, LOSO cross-validation) and\nMELD [31] (7-class, standard train/dev/test split). The cross-dataset\ngeneralization experiment (Section 4.4) transfers MELD-trained\nmodels to IEMOCAP, RAVDESS [24], and SAVEE [16] in a zeroshot manner, and compares HyPASE against the Qwen2-Audio\nzero-shot baseline under the same WA metric.\n\n4.3\n\nAblation Study\n\nAll ablation experiments report WA/UA/F1 on the MELD test set.\nTable 2 decomposes component contributions along the module,\nloss, and geometry dimensions.\nGeometry effect. Comparing M1 (Hyperbolic HGA only) with G2\n(Euclidean HGA only), both using LCE and no EMCA, isolates the\neffect of the parameter space. Hyperbolic weight-space modulation\nyields +2.09 pp WA (66.16 vs. 64.07), indicating a stronger inductive\nbias for audio encoder adaptation than its Euclidean counterpart.\n\nBaselines. We compare against baselines at three levels, following [21]. This single-backbone, MELD+IEMOCAP evaluation protocol is the prevailing paradigm in LALM-based SER, adopted by\nrecent works including EMO-RL [21] and EmoAgent-R1 [12], ensuring cross-study comparability. Non-LALM methods include HuBERT large [18], WavLM large [6], data2vec 2.0 large [1], Whisper\nlarge V3 [32], and Emotion2vec+ large [26], all fine-tuned with classification heads; results are taken from EmoBox [25]. LALM zeroshot baselines use Qwen2-Audio with direct inference and chainof-thought prompting. Euclidean PEFT baselines use LoRA [19]\nand Adapter [17] on the same Qwen2-Audio backbone with identical training configurations as HyPASE, each with approximately\n10.49M trainable parameters. The full-parameter SFT+IR reference\n\nGeometric supervision effect. Adding EMCA to HGA without\ngeometric losses (L0, 63.90) drops below HGA-only performance\n(M1, 66.16). Without radius ordering supervision, the three parallel\nbranches produce redundant representations that interfere with\nHGA’s outputs. Among individual loss terms, Lradius (L2, 65.61)\ncontributes the most by establishing the branch-separation prior;\nLhyp (L1, 64.17) alone provides marginal gain but combines with\nLradius to anchor the ordered structure to emotion semantics. The\n6\n\n\fHyPASE: Hyperbolic Geometry for Parameter-Efficient Speech Emotion Fine-Tuning Framework for Large Audio-Language Models\n\nTable 1: Comparison on MELD (7-class) and IEMOCAP (4-class) (%). Non-LALM results from EmoBox [25]. LALM+PEFT methods\nshare identical training configurations. †: full-parameter reference from [21]. Bold: best PEFT.\nModel Type\n\nMELD\n\nMethod\nUA\n\nWA\n\nF1\n\nUA\n\nWA\n\nF1\n\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\nNon-LALM\n\nHuBERT large\nWavLM large\ndata2vec 2.0 large\nWhisper large V3\nEmotion2vec+ large\n\nNon-LALM Methods\n24.13\n46.37\n28.18\n49.31\n26.33\n47.72\n31.54\n51.89\n28.03\n51.88\n\n24.99\n29.11\n27.35\n32.95\n–\n\n67.42\n69.47\n57.30\n73.54\n70.70\n\n66.69\n69.07\n56.23\n72.86\n67.30\n\n67.24\n69.29\n56.70\n73.11\n–\