ull reproducibility.\n\n4.6.1 Verification of Hyperbolic Structure in Audio Encoder Representations. Audio encoder representations evolve from coarsegrained acoustic patterns (smaller hyperbolic radius) to fine-grained\nemotion semantics (larger radius) in the Poincaré ball, motivating\nHGA’s radius modulation. We compute the Gromov 𝛿-hyperbolicity\nindex (smaller 𝛿 indicates geometry closer to tree-like hyperbolic\nstructure) of representations from each layer of the Qwen2-Audio\naudio encoder and compare with the 𝛿 values of random Gaussian\nvectors of the same dimension. At all sampled layers, 𝛿 audio is substantially smaller than the random baseline 𝛿 random (full per-layer\nplot in Appendix E), The ratio 𝛿 audio /𝛿 random reaches as low as 0.014\nat layer 15, meaning audio representations are approximately 70\ntimes closer to hyperbolic geometry than random vectors; even at\nthe layer with the highest ratio (layer 27), the ratio is only 0.273,\nindicating that hyperbolic structure pervades the entire encoder\nand providing direct empirical evidence for HyPASE’s choice of\nD𝑐𝑑𝑎 as the adaptation space.\n\nAcknowledgments\nThis work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China under Grant No. 62201389, and by the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities under Grant\nNo. 22120230311.\n\n4.6.2 Layer-Adaptive Hyperbolic Radius Modulation by HGA. Figure 3 shows the distribution of diagonal scaling parameters s (ℓ )\n(Equation (5)) learned by HGA after HyPASE training. Scaling factors vary substantially across layers rather than converging to a\nuniform constant. Shallow layers (ℓ < 10) tend toward smaller\nscaling factors, while deep layers (ℓ > 20) tend toward larger ones,\nconsistent with the geometric prediction of Proposition 3.2. This\ngradient confirms that HGA learns layer-adaptive radius modulation aligned with the encoder’s representational hierarchy.\n\n5\n\nConclusion\n\nA\n\nProofs of Theoretical Results\n\nWe prove Theorem 1 and Proposition 1 from the main paper. Both\nproofs rely on the following unifying lemma, which identifies the\nhyperbolic radius of an exp-mapped tangent vector with the Euclidean norm of that vector.\nLemma A.1 (Tangent–Radius Identity). For every v ∈ 𝑇0 D𝑐𝑑 \u001b\nR𝑑 ,\n\nLimitations and Broader Impact\n\nWhile HyPASE demonstrates robust generalization, future work\nshould validate transferability beyond the Qwen2-Audio backbone\n\nRadexp𝐷,𝑐 (v) = ∥v∥.\n0\n\n8\n\n(14)\n\n\fHyPASE: Hyperbolic Geometry for Parameter-Efficient Speech Emotion Fine-Tuning Framework for Large Audio-Language Models\n\nCorollary A.5 (HGA forward-pass simplification). The HGA\n(ℓ )\n(ℓ ) \u0001\n(ℓ ) ⊙ w ,\nEuclidean weight matrix satisfies WHGA,𝑖\n= log𝐷,𝑐\n𝑖\n0 w𝐷,𝑖 = s\ni.e., the full exp–Möbius–log pipeline reduces to element-wise scaling\nof the frozen weight rows in tangent space.\n\nProof. The exponential map at the origin has the closed form\n\u0010√\n\u0011 v\n𝑐\nexp𝐷,𝑐\n,\n(15)\n0 (v) = tanh 2 ∥v∥ √\n𝑐 ∥v∥\nso its Euclidean norm is\n\u0011\n\u0010√\n1\n𝑐\nexp𝐷,𝑐\n0 (v) = √ tanh 2 ∥v∥ .\n𝑐\n\n(ℓ )\n(ℓ ) ⊙\nProof. From the proof of Proposition A.3, w𝐷,𝑖\n= exp𝐷,𝑐\n0 (s\n𝐷,𝑐\n𝐷,𝑐\n(ℓ\n)\nw𝑖 ). Applying log0 (the exact inverse of exp0 ) gives s ⊙ w𝑖\n√\nSubstituting into the hyperbolic radius definition Radx = √2𝑐 artanh( 𝑐 ∥x∥)directly.\n□\n\n(16)\n\n√\n𝑐\n\nand setting 𝑡 := 2 ∥v∥:\n\nB\n\n\u0010√ tanh(𝑡) \u0011\n\u0001\n2\n2\nRadexp𝐷,𝑐 (v) = √ artanh 𝑐 · √\n= √ artanh tanh(𝑡)\n0\n𝑐\n𝑐\n𝑐\n√\n2\n2\n𝑐\n= √ ·𝑡 = √ ·\n∥v∥ = ∥v∥,\n(17)\n𝑐\n𝑐 2\n\nTable 5 provides a self-contained reference for all hyperbolic operations used in HyPASE. Notation follows the main paper throughout;\n𝑐 > 0 is the curvature parameter.\n\nwhere the third equality uses artanh(tanh(𝑡)) = 𝑡 for all 𝑡 ∈ R. □\n□\n\nTable 5: Hyperbolic operations used in HyPASE.\nOperation\n\nThe logarithmic map at the origin is the exact inverse: log𝐷,𝑐\n0 (x) =\n\n√\n√2 artanh( 𝑐 ∥x∥) x , so\n∥x∥\n𝑐\n\n√\n√2\n∥ log𝐷,𝑐\n0 