\nsweeps (Table 7) reveal hard cliff edges at 𝜏≥0.2 and 𝜆𝑟 =0.02, where\nlogit flatness or branch collapse degrades WA to near-chance levels\n(∼30%). Practitioners should therefore fix 𝜏=0.07 and 𝜆𝑟 ∈{0.005, 0.01}\nfirst, then optionally refine 𝜆hyp over the safe range {0.05, 0.1, 0.2};\nthe joint grid search reported here confirms that any combination\nin this subspace produces stable, near-optimal results.\n\nD\n\nE\n\nEMCA total\nHGA (𝑟 =12, 𝐿=32 layers, Q+V)\nFull HyPASE (excl. projector)\n\nHyperparameter Sensitivity\n\nAll sensitivity experiments use MELD (7-class) as the test bed. Table 7 sweeps four key parameters on an isolated EMCA branch\n(without HGA; 𝜏=0.1 code default). Table 8 shows the full 𝜆hyp ×\n𝜆radius grid on the complete HyPASE (HGA enabled; 𝜏=0.1, baseline\nWA 65.52%, cf. main paper WA 68.97% which uses 𝜏=0.07).\n\nFigure 4 reports the full per-layer Gromov 𝛿-hyperbolicity comparison referenced in the main paper (Section 4.5): the true audio\nrepresentation 𝛿 audio against the random Gaussian baseline 𝛿 random\nat every sampled layer of the Qwen2-Audio audio encoder.\n\nValue\n\nWA\n\nUA\n\nCurvature 𝑐 (def. 0.01)\n\n0.003\n0.03\n0.1\n\n46.17\n58.16\n30.38\n\n45.25 flat; near-Euclidean\n46.12 workable\n41.04 collapse; too curved\n\nTemperature 𝜏 (def. 0.1)\n\n0.05\n0.2\n0.4\n\n65.21 43.16 near-full performance\n29.27 41.36 logit flatness\n31.15 42.07 logit flatness\n\n𝜆hyp (def. 0.1)\n\n0.0\n0.05\n0.2\n\n44.29\n33.03\n30.15\n\n45.30 no prototype loss\n42.92 worse than 0.0\n41.53 over-regularised\n\n𝜆radius (def. 0.01)\n\n0.0\n0.005\n0.02\n\n49.69\n60.08\n30.04\n\n45.99 no branch ordering\n42.44 recovers most gain\n41.55 collapse; too rigid\n\n-hyperbolicity\n\nTable 7: Single-factor sensitivity on EMCA-only branch (HGA\ndisabled). WA/UA/wF1 (%).\nParam\n\nPer-Layer Gromov delta-Hyperbolicity\n\nNote\n\n0.08\n0.07\n0.06\n0.05\n0.04\n0.03\n0.02\n0.01\n0.00\n\nAudio features\n\n0\n\n3\n\n7\n\n11\n\nRandom baseline\n\n15\n\nLayer\n\n19\n\n23\n\n27\n\n31\n\nFigure 4: Gromov 𝛿-hyperbolicity of each Qwen2-Audio layer\nvs. random Gaussian baseline on MELD. Audio representations at all layers exhibit markedly lower 𝛿 than random\nvectors, confirming inherent hyperbolic structure.\n\nKey findings. (1) Among all single-factor sweeps, 𝜏 and 𝜆radius\nare the most dangerous: both collapse to near-random (∼30% WA)\nwhen set too large (𝜏 ≥ 0.2 or 𝜆𝑟 =0.02). (2) In the full HyPASE grid,\n15/16 configurations achieve WA within ±0.7 pp of the baseline,\nconfirming that HGA provides robust geometric regularization that\ncushions lambda sensitivity. (3) The sole outlier (𝜆ℎ =0.05, 𝜆𝑟 =0.005,\n\nReferences\n[1] Alexei Baevski, Wei-Ning Hsu, Alexis Conneau, and Michael Auli. 2023. data2vec\n2.0: Highly Efficient Self-Supervised Learning for Vision, Speech, and Text. arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2212.07525 (2023).\n[2] Rainer Banse and Klaus R. Scherer. 1996. Acoustic profiles in vocal emotion\nexpression. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology 70, 3 (1996), 614–636.\ndoi:10.1037/0022-3514.70.3.614\n10\n\n\fHyPASE: Hyperbolic Geometry for Parameter-Efficient Speech Emotion Fine-Tuning Framework for Large Audio-Language Models\n\n[22] Xiang Lisa Li and Percy Liang. 2021. Prefix-Tuning: Optimizing Continuous\nPrompts for Generation. In Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational\nLinguistics (ACL). 4582–4597. doi:10.18653/v1/2021.acl-long.353\n[23] Zhipeng Li, Xiaofen Xing, Yuanbo Fang, Weibin Zhang, Hengsheng Fan, and\nXiangmin Xu. 2024. Multi-Scale Temporal Transformer For Speech Emotion\nRecognition. arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.00390 (2024). https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.\n00390\n[24] Steven R. Livingstone and Frank A. Russo. 2018. The Ryerson Audio-Visual\nDatabase of Emotional Speech and Song (RAVDESS): A Dynamic, Multimodal\nSet of Facial and Vocal Expressions in North American English. PLoS ONE 13, 5\n(2018), e0196391.\n[25] Ziyang Ma, Zhisheng Zheng, Jiaxin Ye, Jinchao Li, Zhifu Gao, Shiliang Zhang, and\nXie Chen. 2024.